  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nWarmer temperatures and dry weather are expected for Friday with\nupper level ridging in place. A weak shortwave will track in from\nthe west for isolated to widely scattered showers and\nthunderstorms Friday night.\n\nAttention next turns to Saturday afternoon into Saturday night\nwhen the next round of strong to severe thunderstorms appears to\nbe in the cards across much of NE Montana. A strong upper level\ntrough will track east with diffluent southwest upper flow over\neastern Montana. HREF shows the timing of initiation roughly \nafter 4 PM with storms tracking northeast during the evening \nhours. Surface CAPE is the strongest, 1500-2000 J/KG near the \nMT/ND border, along with around 40 kts of 0-6 km shear. Forecast \nsoundings show inverted-v signatures in the area by Saturday \nafternoon with relatively moist condition above 500mb. \nPrecipitable water values look to range between 1.00 and 1.25 with\nsurface dew points in the 50s to near 60 with southeast low level\nflow in the east where the greatest risk likely will be. Overall \nwould expect this profile to support multicellular and \nsupercellular structures supportive of large hail, damaging winds,\nbrief heavy rainfall, and frequent lightning. \n\nThis trough will shift east on Sunday with lingering showers and\nthunderstorms along with cooler temperatures. As the trough pushes\ndownstream look for a gradual drying trend Monday into Tuesday\nwith moderating temperatures.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nNBM was in charge this forecast cycle which captured expected\ntrends well for the 7 day period. A blend with high resolution\nmodel solutions may be appropriate for pops in the next couple of\ndays to better capture mesoscale evolution of expected\nthunderstorms for Saturday and Sunday.\n\nConfidence is increasing on strong to severe thunderstorms for\nSaturday afternoon and evening, with the highest risk area from a\nline through about Glasgow to Jordan eastward to the MT/ND border.",
